 "raw_reply": "{\n  \"experiment_id\": \"EXP-004\",\n  \"observed_evidence\": [\n    {\n      \"statement\": \"The selection rule is mean ROC-AUC minus 0.25×fold standard deviation, then runtime, on training CV only.\",\n      \"citation\": \"EXP-004 / evidence.selection_rule\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"statement\": \"LightGBM has mean ROC-AUC 0.89555 and fold std 0.00645 over 3 folds with 1 repeat. This matches the claim's 0.8955±0.0065. The stage is raw and the optimization is baseline.\",\n      \"citation\": \"EXP-004 / evidence.model_results[model=lightgbm].metrics.roc_auc\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"statement\": \"HistGradientBoosting has mean ROC-AUC 0.89532 and std 0.00893. The mean gap to LightGBM is about 0.0002.\",\n      \"citation\": \"EXP-004 / evidence.model_results[model=hist_gradient_boosting].metrics.roc_auc\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"statement\": \"XGBoost has mean ROC-AUC 0.89196 (std 0.00833), ExtraTrees 0.88914 (std 0.00758), and logistic regression 0.84697 (std 0.00467).\",\n      \"citation\": \"EXP-004 / evidence.model_results[model=xgboost|extra_trees|logistic_regression].metrics.roc_auc\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"statement\": \"Per-fold ROC-AUC for LightGBM is 0.89205, 0.89160 and 0.90300. For HistGradientBoosting it is 0.88882, 0.89163 and 0.90551. LightGBM leads in fold 1 and trails by a negligible amount in fold 2 and by about 0.0025 in fold 3.\",\n      \"citation\": \"EXP-004 / evidence.model_results[model=lightgbm|hist_gradient_boosting].fold_metrics[].roc_auc\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"statement\": \"LightGBM mean ECE_10 is 0.0578 and HistGradientBoosting is 0.0555. Logistic regression is 0.0195, and ExtraTrees is 0.0253.\",\n      \"citation\": \"EXP-004 / evidence.model_results[].metrics.ece_10.mean\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"statement\": \"ExtraTrees has the lowest mean log loss (0.3547) and mean Brier (0.1093). LightGBM has 0.3560 and 0.1099.\",\n      \"citation\": \"EXP-004 / evidence.model_results[model=extra_trees|lightgbm].metrics.log_loss.mean, metrics.brier.mean\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"statement\": \"All fold metrics use a fixed threshold of 0.5.\",\n      \"citation\": \"EXP-004 / evidence.model_results[].fold_metrics[].threshold\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"statement\": \"LightGBM's highest mean normalized importance is fnlwgt (0.258), followed by age (0.191). Sex and race are among the 14 raw features. HistGradientBoosting reports no feature_stability entries.\",\n      \"citation\": \"EXP-004 / evidence.model_results[model=lightgbm|hist_gradient_boosting].feature_stability\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"statement\": \"The bundle's limitations list is empty. Preprocessing, split stratification, duplicate handling, and dataset size are not reported.\",\n      \"citation\": \"EXP-004 / limitations\"\n    }\n  ],\n  \"interpretation\": \"Fact: by the stated rule, LightGBM scores about 0.8939 (0.89555 − 0.25×0.00645) and HistGradientBoosting about 0.8931. The claim's number is therefore reproducible and LightGBM nominally ranks first among the five baselines. Inference: the gap to HistGradientBoosting is about 0.0002 in mean ROC-AUC. That is far smaller than either model's fold std, and the per-fold sign flips. With 3 folds and 1 repeat, the two are statistically indistinguishable. The evidence supports 'gradient-boosted trees (LightGBM and HistGradientBoosting) beat linear and ExtraTrees baselines in training CV', not a single stable winner. 'Stable' is also only weakly supported, because logistic regression has the lowest std (0.0047) but a much lower mean. The ranking uses ROC-AUC only. ExtraTrees leads on log loss and Brier, and logistic regression leads on ECE, so the leader depends on the metric. Hypothesis (not tested): LightGBM's high fnlwgt importance may reflect noise-fitting or sampling-weight artifacts rather than useful signal. Importance is not causal and is not evidence of predictive validity.\",\n  \"decision\": \"Keep LightGBM as a provisional training-CV candidate, tied with HistGradientBoosting, pending repeated paired CV. Do not call it the strongest or a stable winner. Make no deployment claim and do not touch the locked holdout. Do not approve any feature (including fnlwgt, sex, race, native-country) without decision-time and production-availability review.\",\n  \"risks_and_missing_evidence\": [\n    \"Only 3 folds and 1 repeat, so fold std and CI95 are very noisy. The 0.25×std penalty is not a reliable stability measure, and the ranking margin is within noise.\",\n    \"All models are untuned baselines. Tuning budgets and defaults differ across families, so the result may reflect default hyperparameters rather than algorithm family strength.\",\n    \"Preprocessing is not documented. Encoders, imputation, and category handling must be fit inside the training folds, and the handling of categoricals differs across the compared models.\",\n    \"Split design is not reported: stratification, grouping, and duplicate-row handling. Duplicate rows are common in Adult-style data and can inflate CV if split across folds.\",\n    \"The dataset size is not given, so the sample behind the folds is unknown.\",\n    \"fnlwgt is a census sampling weight. Its decision-time meaning and production availability are unreviewed, and its high LightGBM importance is a risk flag, not a finding.\",\n    \"Sex, race, native-country and marital-status/relationship are included as raw features. There is no fairness or legal and production-availability review, and no subgroup metrics are reported.\",\n    \"The default 0.5 threshold is used for accuracy, F1, precision and recall. These are not comparable across models with different calibration, and ECE is notably worse for boosted models (about 0.058-0.067) than for logistic regression (0.0195).\",\n    \"The empty feature_stability for HistGradientBoosting prevents comparing the top-two models' feature reliance.\",\n    \"Importance values are model-specific and split-based for LightGBM, with different scales across families. They are not comparable and not causal.\",\n    \"No holdout evidence, as the claim itself notes. The bundle's limitations list is empty, which understates the claim-level limitations.\"\n  ],\n  \"challenges_to_claims\": [\n    {\n      \"claim_id\": \"EXP-004-C1\",\n      \"issue\": \"The LightGBM lead over HistGradientBoosting (about 0.0002 mean ROC-AUC, inconsistent per-fold sign) is within fold noise from 3 folds and 1 repeat. Naming a single candidate overstates the evidence, and HistGradientBoosting is effectively tied.\",\n      \"severity\": \"high\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"claim_id\": \"EXP-004-C1\",\n      \"issue\": \"The question asks for 'strongest stable' evidence, but stability rests on a 3-fold std. HistGradientBoosting and LightGBM stds differ by only about 0.0025 and are themselves unreliable estimates.\",\n      \"severity\": \"medium\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"claim_id\": \"EXP-004-C1\",\n      \"issue\": \"The ranking uses ROC-AUC alone. ExtraTrees has better log loss and Brier, and logistic regression has much better ECE, so 'best' is metric-dependent. If probabilities will be used, calibration matters.\",\n      \"severity\": \"medium\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"claim_id\": \"EXP-004-C1\",\n      \"issue\": \"The claim says 'raw features' without any decision-time or production-availability review. LightGBM leans heavily on fnlwgt, and sex and race are included, so the candidate is not feature-approved.\",\n      \"severity\": \"high\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"claim_id\": \"EXP-004-C1\",\n      \"issue\": \"Baseline-only models (no tuning) cannot support a conclusion about algorithm family strength, only about default configurations.\",\n      \"severity\": \"medium\"\n    }\n  ],\n  \"next_experiment\": {\n    \"title\": \"Paired repeated stratified CV: LightGBM vs HistGradientBoosting on identical splits (training data only)\",\n    \"hypothesis\": \"If LightGBM is genuinely better than HistGradientBoosting on raw features, its paired ROC-AUC advantage will be positive and consistent across 5x repeated stratified 5-fold CV on identical, de-duplicated splits. If the two are tied, the paired difference interval will include zero.\",\n    \"success_gate\": \"LightGBM is retained as sole candidate only if (a) the mean paired ROC-AUC difference (LightGBM minus HistGradientBoosting) over the 25 folds is > 0, (b) the 95% interval for that difference, computed over per-repeat means to respect dependence, excludes 0, and (c) LightGBM wins in at least 70% of folds. Otherwise declare a tie and break it using log loss, ECE and runtime. Report fnlwgt-in and fnlwgt-out ROC-AUC as a sensitivity check without approving either.\",\n    \"why_smallest\": \"It changes only the evaluation protocol (more repeats, shared splits, de-duplication) for the two leading models. It adds no new models, tuning or features, directly tests whether the observed gap is noise, and does not touch the holdout.\"\n  },\n  \"overall_confidence\": \"medium\"\n}",
